Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Euclidean Infrageometry", "GuideTitle",
 CellID->942062912,ExpressionUUID->"a3a3c038-ca24-47b7-9945-83f1c1312fe3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Euclidean Infrageometry",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " studies natural geometric objects on arbitrary graph substrates using ",
 StyleBox["synthetic methods",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". It enumerates these objects, studies their mutual relations, applies \
operations to them, and performs iterative constructions. If space is \
represented by a large graph, which we may view as a web of paths, human \
geometry can be viewed as an idealized theory based on our large-scale \
perception of ",
 StyleBox["infinitesimality",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". This leads to the idealization of space as a continuous \"web\" \
containing idealized objects such as the \"breadthless line.\" Infrageometry \
works directly with the discrete substrate at an observer's scale and does \
not benefit from infinitesimality. Consequently, objects may not be unique, \
leading to ",
 StyleBox["multi-objects",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", and constructions generate branching ",
 StyleBox["multiway systems",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". Although concrete representatives may differ across branches, the system \
is \"causally invariant\" in the sense that the dependency graph of \
construction steps is the same in every branch. We associate each \
multi-object with ",
 StyleBox["vertex and edge densities",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " that count the representatives containing each vertex or edge. Dividing by \
the number of representatives gives the corresponding fractions. We expect \
that, under a suitable notion of ",
 StyleBox["Gromov-Hausdorff convergence",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", these measures converge weakly to Dirac measures supported on classical \
\"thin\" geometric objects, provided the limiting space supports the \
classical Euclidean axioms."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["", "GuideFunctionsSection",
 CellID->1866139230,ExpressionUUID->"a0ea3657-4c83-4862-93c8-ecf80cebc774"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We use ",
 StyleBox["inert symbolic objects",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " such as ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"InfraSegment", "[",
   RowBox[{"p", ",", " ", "q"}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "14e225f4-554a-4cd3-9cbf-74577ffe6b1f"],
 ", independent of a graph substrate. ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"InfraMeasurement", "[",
   RowBox[{"g", ",", " ", "obj", ",", " ", "property"}], "]"}]],
  "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "4c343921-99fc-478b-b65d-d79a5bccbbd1"],
 " reads an object on a graph. Each construction has a named sampler; for \
example, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"RandomInfraSegment", "[",
   RowBox[{"g", ",", " ",
    RowBox[{"InfraSegment", "[",
     RowBox[{"p", ",", " ", "q"}], "]"}]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "7154a2a6-f4d7-4aab-9db4-140bded9286f"],
 " draws one shortest-path vertex sequence. See ",
 ButtonBox["Named construction samplers",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["../Tutorials/NamedConstructionSamplers.md"], None}],
 " for the family-specific carriers and overloads."
}], "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"75e747e4-3136-43fb-92e0-721ba52d3637"],

Cell["\<\
Segments, rays, lines and arcs have compact graph representations. Their \
paths encode the representatives, allowing counts and densities to be \
computed without enumerating every path. These graphs are part of the \
Euclidean construction tools.\
\>", "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"0ec2def0-413f-4b0c-a856-f9ffbe1ec75e"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "An ",
 Cell[BoxData["InfraScene"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "b4341b7b-6486-40ef-afa7-550643b25de2"],
 " describes a construction through named objects and hypotheses. ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"RandomInfraInstance", "[",
   RowBox[{"scene", ",", " ", "graph"}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "22345928-4316-4fd4-8edc-437b1c4a29bf"],
 " draws an admissible instance on a substrate, and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"InfraSceneViewer", "[",
   RowBox[{"scene", ",", " ", "graph"}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "21edb2ca-3b1d-4e13-863a-1cb62b08dbe4"],
 " displays the construction step by step."
}], "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"2155619a-053e-4b4f-beae-5f4f594e0e51"],

